  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/execution_coverage/progress.rs#mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[write_line]` meets the built-in's criteria.\n\n- **Placement:** It is on an explicit function with a body, and its explanation is in fenced citation commentary. That is the form the built-in permits.\n- **Substantive reason:** The commentary does more than say the item is hard to cover. It says `RecordingTerm` is a unit-test-only adapter, that indicatif renders through `write_str`, and that `TermLike` merely requires `write_line`. It also says the function's only behavior is to delegate to `write_str`, and that no application input can reach this callback.\n- **Agreement with the source:**\n  - `write_line` is just `self.write_str(&format!(\"{text}\\n\"))`, and `write_str` is exercised by the tests, which read the recorded text and frames.\n  - `RecordingTerm` is inside `#[cfg(test)] mod tests`, so it is unit-test-only.\n  - Every `Progress` display uses a single-line template, with `{spinner} {msg} [{elapsed_precise}]`, optionally followed by a bar. Indicatif's term-like drawing, as I recall it, calls `write_line` only for non-final lines of multi-line draws, so a single-line display does not reach it. I did not read the indicatif 0.18.4 source to confirm this.\n  - The sibling commentary on the other no-op callbacks makes the same single-line assumption.\n  - The production target is `console::Term`, not this adapter. The commentary's claim that real terminal output is exercised on a PTY relies on a test I did not open.\n- **Scope:** The exception covers only this function's unexecuted coverage. It does not excuse the file's threshold.\n\nI found no gap.",
